WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.170 --> 00:00:04.430
The ending of the original Night Court tied
up loose ends and featured a very special

00:00:04.430 --> 00:00:07.710
guest spot from a jazz singer known as The
Velvet Fog.

00:00:07.710 --> 00:00:11.769
Here's how the long-running sitcom left the
airwaves on a high note.

00:00:11.952 --> 00:00:16.942
Night Court didn't have to wait three decades
for the events of its final episodes to be undone.

00:00:16.942 --> 00:00:23.002
It only took a couple of weeks, a stray unaired
episode, and a hole in the NBC programming schedule.

00:00:23.002 --> 00:00:27.619
The series finale was a two-parter titled
"Opportunity Knock Knocks," which aired in

00:00:27.619 --> 00:00:31.210
a one-hour block on Wednesday, May 13, 1992.

00:00:31.210 --> 00:00:36.190
The episodes were promoted on NBC as the end
of an era, and the episodes could not be mistaken

00:00:36.190 --> 00:00:39.920
for anything but a show's final bow in both
form and content.

00:00:39.920 --> 00:00:44.510
Yet, on a Sunday night more than two weeks
later, what does NBC put on the air?

00:00:44.510 --> 00:00:46.820
A brand new episode of Night Court.

00:00:46.820 --> 00:00:51.180
Sunday evening programming on NBC in those
years was a hodge-podge of special events,

00:00:51.180 --> 00:00:53.420
movies-of-the-week, and sitcom reruns.

00:00:53.420 --> 00:00:58.190
This particular episode, "The 1992 Boat Show,"
was produced earlier in the season, but for

00:00:58.190 --> 00:01:01.180
whatever reason was knocked from its planned
premiere date.

00:01:01.180 --> 00:01:05.920
Directed by series regular Charles "Mac" Robinson,
the episode sees another one of Dan's social

00:01:05.920 --> 00:01:10.229
climbing schemes blow up in his face when
he gets a fancy columnist out of jury duty

00:01:10.229 --> 00:01:14.040
in exchange for a write-up in the society
pages — only for the reporter to become

00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:16.307
entranced with Roz instead.

00:01:16.691 --> 00:01:21.100
For anyone paying attention in 1992, that
extra episode of Night Court must have been

00:01:21.100 --> 00:01:22.650
doubly confusing.

00:01:22.650 --> 00:01:26.890
Not only had the previous two episodes been
explicitly billed as the end of the show,

00:01:26.890 --> 00:01:30.650
but they sent several of the series' main
characters off into the sunset — only for

00:01:30.650 --> 00:01:34.750
them to pop up in court once again in "Boat
Show" as if nothing had happened.

00:01:34.750 --> 00:01:38.899
Networks deciding to air episodes out of sequence
was, of course, nothing new at the time and

00:01:38.899 --> 00:01:43.430
remains a questionable practice even today
when shows are far more serialized in general

00:01:43.430 --> 00:01:44.759
than they used to be.

00:01:44.759 --> 00:01:48.939
Plot-wise, the finale marks the conclusion
of two ongoing storylines that were begun

00:01:48.939 --> 00:01:50.720
a few episodes earlier.

00:01:50.720 --> 00:01:55.080
These include Christine's surprisingly difficult
congressional race against a deeply unserious

00:01:55.080 --> 00:01:59.719
opponent and Harry's decision to step down
from the bench and become a law professor.

00:01:59.719 --> 00:02:03.430
More importantly, though, the episodes resolve
emotional journeys that have been going on

00:02:03.430 --> 00:02:08.220
since nearly the first episode, from Harry
and Christine's see-sawing romantic relationship

00:02:08.220 --> 00:02:09.420
to Dan's womanizing.

00:02:09.420 --> 00:02:14.170
However, all of this works together to examine
something even more important — the general

00:02:14.170 --> 00:02:18.690
feeling that the night court is a somewhat
melancholy place, full of people who got there

00:02:18.690 --> 00:02:21.508
by happenstance and then never left.

00:02:21.651 --> 00:02:26.771
As Harry weighs his options about whether
to leave the bench, job offers start pouring in.

00:02:26.771 --> 00:02:29.620
First, it is a professorship at Columbia Law.

00:02:29.620 --> 00:02:32.180
Then, a partnership with a prestigious white
shoe firm.

00:02:32.180 --> 00:02:35.190
Then, a possible judgeship in the superior
court.

00:02:35.190 --> 00:02:38.090
Then, somehow, a sponsorship deal with Nike.

00:02:38.090 --> 00:02:42.500
Just when things start to feel a little ridiculous,
jazz singer Mel Tormé, nicknamed The Velvet

00:02:42.500 --> 00:02:48.280
Fog, bursts into Harry's office with an offer
to go on tour and work on his next album together.

00:02:48.280 --> 00:02:52.520
As goofball and non-sequitur as the show's
humor could be, Tormé didn't pop out of thin

00:02:52.520 --> 00:02:53.659
air for the finale.

00:02:53.659 --> 00:02:57.970
Instead, his appearance is the capper for
dozens of jokes and cameos going all the way

00:02:57.970 --> 00:03:03.800
back to Season 1, when Tormé superfan Harry
staged a one-person concert in his chambers.

00:03:03.800 --> 00:03:08.110
The 1980s saw a wave of nostalgia for the
Big Band era that made stars out of young

00:03:08.110 --> 00:03:12.780
bandleaders like Wynton Marsalis and Harry
Connick, Jr. while shining a fresh spotlight

00:03:12.780 --> 00:03:15.690
on survivors like Tormé and Tony Bennett.

00:03:15.690 --> 00:03:20.911
Tormé eventually guested on the show nine
times, usually as himself or as Harry's guardian angel.

00:03:20.911 --> 00:03:24.720
Though the aging crooner's role was always
tongue-in-cheek, the show's appreciation of

00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:26.801
him was absolutely genuine.

00:03:26.801 --> 00:03:32.420
Lead actor Harry Anderson had been a lifelong
fan even before the series and spoke at Tormé's

00:03:32.420 --> 00:03:34.277
funeral in 1999.

00:03:34.581 --> 00:03:38.720
As Harry weighs his options for which of his
many ludicrous job offers to take, he has

00:03:38.720 --> 00:03:42.840
written each of them down on an index card
tacked to a small corkboard.

00:03:42.840 --> 00:03:47.670
He looks at the board, pauses, then turns
it away from the camera and the audience.

00:03:47.670 --> 00:03:51.450
He plays eeny-meeny-miny-moe, landing on the
decision to teach at Columbia, and when he

00:03:51.450 --> 00:03:55.990
turns the board back around, all of the cards
now read the same thing — Columbia.

00:03:55.990 --> 00:03:59.210
"Unanimous vote."

00:03:59.210 --> 00:04:03.510
It's a fun little moment, one of many throughout
the run of the series that indulged Anderson's

00:04:03.510 --> 00:04:05.310
skill at sleight-of-hand.

00:04:05.310 --> 00:04:09.590
As a stand-up comic, card tricks and a general
old-timey vibe were hallmarks of his act and

00:04:09.590 --> 00:04:12.780
stage persona both before and after Night
Court.

00:04:12.780 --> 00:04:17.420
His most notable role prior to the series
was as small-time Boston con man Harry the

00:04:17.420 --> 00:04:18.949
Hat on Cheers.

00:04:18.949 --> 00:04:24.450
Decked out in a fedora and a wide 1940s-style
necktie, Harry the Hat was a welcome nuisance,

00:04:24.450 --> 00:04:28.370
running small games at the bar and often getting
the best of gullible characters like Coach

00:04:28.370 --> 00:04:29.370
and Woody.

00:04:29.473 --> 00:04:34.560
Like his contemporary Sam Malone on Cheers,
John Larroquette's lecherous lawyer Dan Fielding

00:04:34.560 --> 00:04:37.500
is the kind of character that wouldn't really
fly today.

00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:41.620
The show ran long enough to provide Dan with
layers underneath his boorish exterior — and

00:04:41.620 --> 00:04:45.560
Larroquette's performance was so good that
it was easy to enjoy the character without

00:04:45.560 --> 00:04:48.080
fully acknowledging his worst behavior.

00:04:48.080 --> 00:04:52.160
Dan is often the butt of the joke, and his
schemes more often than not blow up in his

00:04:52.160 --> 00:04:57.070
face, but at the same time he mostly gets
a free pass for his callous treatment of women.

00:04:57.070 --> 00:05:01.280
To its credit, though, the finale reckons
with Dan's womanizing not once but twice,

00:05:01.280 --> 00:05:03.100
in both dramatic and farcical fashion.

00:05:03.100 --> 00:05:08.040
In "Opportunity Knock Knocks (Part 1)," Dan's
plans to propose to a wealthy publishing heiress

00:05:08.040 --> 00:05:12.810
are dashed when he discovers that 25 years
earlier he'd broken the heart of his would-be

00:05:12.810 --> 00:05:15.520
fianceé's mother, played by Christine Belford.

00:05:15.520 --> 00:05:19.479
Belford delivers the revelation in a stark
monologue, while Larroquette does some of

00:05:19.479 --> 00:05:22.020
his best work in the series by just listening.

00:05:22.020 --> 00:05:25.800
"Opportunity Knock Knocks (Part 2)," on the
other hand, gives Dan his comeuppance in the

00:05:25.800 --> 00:05:30.430
form of a dream sequence in which every woman
he's ever known packs into the courtroom to

00:05:30.430 --> 00:05:36.397
put him on trial for his misdeeds, with Christine
as his defender and Roz as his heartless judge.

00:05:36.541 --> 00:05:41.639
Richard Moll's breakout bailiff "Bull" Shannon
is but one in a continuum of wacky sitcom

00:05:41.639 --> 00:05:45.770
characters who are not quite of this Earth
— from Christopher Lloyd's burnout Reverend

00:05:45.770 --> 00:05:48.180
Jim on Taxi to Kramer on Seinfeld.

00:05:48.180 --> 00:05:52.930
The finale finds Bull in the grips of paranoia,
convinced that he is being followed by strange

00:05:52.930 --> 00:05:55.350
men who he believes might be aliens.

00:05:55.350 --> 00:06:00.010
When we finally see these "aliens" in "Opportunity
Knock Knocks (Part 2)," they are a pair of

00:06:00.010 --> 00:06:03.190
little old men from Jupiter — Jupiter, Florida,
that is.

00:06:03.190 --> 00:06:07.540
However, at the end of the episode, we see
that Bull was right all along.

00:06:07.540 --> 00:06:11.430
These gentlemen really are from Jupiter the
planet and have come to Earth to conscript

00:06:11.430 --> 00:06:15.979
Bull for a mission of intergalactic importance
— they need him to reach all the high stuff

00:06:15.979 --> 00:06:17.389
on their shelves.

00:06:17.389 --> 00:06:21.370
In an episode full of farewells, Bull's is
perhaps the most permanent, disappearing from

00:06:21.370 --> 00:06:25.050
Harry's darkened office in a flash of light
and a roll of the credits.

00:06:25.050 --> 00:06:28.970
Perhaps that finality is why Moll has been
less than enthusiastic over the years about

00:06:28.970 --> 00:06:31.113
returning to his most famous role.

00:06:31.213 --> 00:06:32.628
“So, will you come?”

00:06:33.963 --> 00:06:35.343
"Oooookay."

00:06:35.861 --> 00:06:40.640
Within a year of the show's final episode
Harry Anderson, Markie Post, and John Larroquette

00:06:40.640 --> 00:06:44.319
were each headlining their own network series
with mixed results.

00:06:44.319 --> 00:06:48.900
Anderson decamped to CBS for the family sitcom
Dave's World, a fictionalized take on the

00:06:48.900 --> 00:06:52.000
life of newspaper columnist and author Dave
Barry.

00:06:52.000 --> 00:06:57.090
Post also moved to CBS, partnering with John
Ritter for the political sitcom Hearts Afire.

00:06:57.090 --> 00:07:01.110
The most successful of these, critically if
not commercially, was The John Larroquette

00:07:01.110 --> 00:07:04.389
Show, which ran for four seasons on NBC.

00:07:04.389 --> 00:07:09.000
Larroquette starred as John Hemingway, a recovering
alcoholic who works the night shift at a St.

00:07:09.000 --> 00:07:10.201
Louis bus terminal.

00:07:10.201 --> 00:07:14.639
While its setup bore similarities to Night
Court — the show was much closer to a dramedy,

00:07:14.639 --> 00:07:18.900
or even a weekly short play, particularly
in its darker Season 1.

00:07:18.900 --> 00:07:23.280
Despite Emmy nominations for Laroquette and
co-star Liz Torres in its first three seasons,

00:07:23.280 --> 00:07:28.492
the show was never a popular success and was
abruptly canceled part-way through its fourth season.

00:07:28.492 --> 00:07:32.828
Several episodes were never aired until released
for syndication years later.

00:07:33.310 --> 00:07:38.250
For fans of Tina Fey's legendary sitcom 30
Rock, it must be both validating and depressing

00:07:38.250 --> 00:07:42.389
that so many of the show's showbiz jokes have
become real life things since the show ended

00:07:42.389 --> 00:07:43.830
in 2013.

00:07:43.830 --> 00:07:48.460
The 2023 Night Court revival could be added
to that list as well, except for the fact

00:07:48.460 --> 00:07:52.151
that 30 Rock didn't just joke about a reunion,
it actually made it happen.

00:07:52.151 --> 00:07:53.360
Sort of.

00:07:53.360 --> 00:07:57.930
Season 3's "The One with the cast of Night
Court" sees Kenneth the NBC page despondent

00:07:57.930 --> 00:07:59.669
over the pages' new uniforms.

00:07:59.669 --> 00:08:03.949
To cheer him up, show-within-a-show star Tracy
Jordan gathers the cast of Night Court in

00:08:03.949 --> 00:08:08.610
order for Kenneth to shoot his unauthorized
and probably-illegal script for a more satisfying

00:08:08.610 --> 00:08:09.740
series finale.

00:08:09.740 --> 00:08:14.270
However, Kenneth's plans to give Harry and
Christine a proper courthouse wedding go awry

00:08:14.270 --> 00:08:18.887
when the actors fall back into the same 15-year-old
jealousies and arguments.

00:08:19.081 --> 00:08:24.050
When the Night Court sequel series was announced
in 2021, fans were justified in wondering

00:08:24.050 --> 00:08:28.210
not only who from the original cast might
return but who from the original cast was

00:08:28.210 --> 00:08:29.970
still alive to return.

00:08:29.970 --> 00:08:33.810
The series suffered two notable deaths while
it was still on the air.

00:08:33.810 --> 00:08:39.039
Original bailiff Selma Diamond died after
the first season in 1985, only for her replacement,

00:08:39.039 --> 00:08:43.140
actress Florence Halop , to die after the
second season a year later.

00:08:43.140 --> 00:08:47.459
After Dave's World ended in 1997, Anderson
stepped away from the limelight, opening a

00:08:47.459 --> 00:08:50.790
jazz club in New Orleans where he performed
comedy and magic.

00:08:50.790 --> 00:08:54.990
He took just a handful of on-camera roles
in the years that followed on shows like Son

00:08:54.990 --> 00:08:56.399
of the Beach.

00:08:56.399 --> 00:09:00.940
In 2018 Anderson died from a stroke in his
North Carolina home at age 65.

00:09:00.940 --> 00:09:05.550
Then, in 2021, both Markie Post and Charles
Robinson passed away.

00:09:05.550 --> 00:09:09.920
Like Anderson, Post had stayed away from being
a series regular after the end of Hearts Afire,

00:09:09.920 --> 00:09:12.950
but had notable roles on Scrubs and Chicago
P.D.

00:09:12.950 --> 00:09:15.500
She died after a brief battle with cancer.

00:09:15.500 --> 00:09:20.149
Robinson was a celebrated stage actor in addition
to his on-screen work and had performed with

00:09:20.149 --> 00:09:24.650
The Wire star Wendell Pierce in a version
of James Anthony Tyler's play "Some Old Black

00:09:24.650 --> 00:09:27.600
Man" just a few months before his death from
a heart attack.

00:09:27.922 --> 00:09:31.970
The new Night Court doesn't ignore the loss
of so many of the original cast members but

00:09:31.970 --> 00:09:36.649
rather places that loss front and center in
the characters of Abby and Dan.

00:09:36.649 --> 00:09:41.240
The in-universe death of Harry Stone hangs
over the entire revival, with Abby using her

00:09:41.240 --> 00:09:46.690
night court judgeship as a way to connect
with a version of her father who she never knew.

00:09:46.889 --> 00:09:48.111
"You're Harry's daughter?

00:09:51.161 --> 00:09:52.061
Come in."

00:09:52.061 --> 00:09:56.550
The widowed Dan, meanwhile, is more worn out
by the length of life in general.

00:09:56.550 --> 00:10:00.920
Age and a great love have sapped his energy,
and his shock at Abby's request for him to

00:10:00.920 --> 00:10:04.710
return to his old job is rooted in a very
relatable feeling.

00:10:04.710 --> 00:10:08.990
After they stopped working together, he and
Harry fell out of touch and did not stay friends.

00:10:08.990 --> 00:10:13.180
It's a surprisingly real comment on the type
of thing that legacy sequels usually ignore

00:10:13.180 --> 00:10:17.890
— life goes on after the last episode, and
the years spent working in a wacky after-hours

00:10:17.890 --> 00:10:22.110
arraignment court end up accounting for just
a brief chapter in that life.

00:10:22.110 --> 00:10:26.230
For now, Larroquette is the only returning
cast member from the original series.

00:10:26.230 --> 00:10:31.959
He anchors a young, diverse cast that includes
India de Beaufort as prosecutor Olivia, comedian

00:10:31.959 --> 00:10:36.780
Lacretta as Gurgs the bailiff, and Kapil Talwalkar
as court officer Neil.

00:10:36.780 --> 00:10:41.191
In January 2023, Marsha Warfield gave the
new show her blessing via Twitter, though

00:10:41.191 --> 00:10:44.990
there is no word yet as to whether she will
play Roz once again.

00:10:44.990 --> 00:10:47.800
Richard Moll, meanwhile, has declined to participate.

00:10:47.800 --> 00:10:51.180
Presumably, Bull is still reaching tall shelves
on Jupiter.

